y unpopular planning  reforms that the uh government is  bringing forward to deprive local people  of a  of any say over developments on their  own streets and in their own  neighborhood so  you know there is a growing coalition  across this country intent  on stopping the conservatives from  continuing uh on the path they're going  along  uh about this country's future  i want to come to the planning issue  because that's an area that i know you  are  uh expert in but let's just talk about  the politics about this  of this for another minute or so did i  understand from what you've just said  that  however informal however unspoken  you think that the possibility  of essentially an anti-tory alliance  uh exists that would see the  different parties the liberal democrats  the greens  the scottish national party essentially  combining  to get the tories out  well i didn't i didn't mean to imply  that at all uh trevor i i'm not myself  attracted you're talking  fundamentally about what's called the  progressive uh alliance so-called  and i'm not attracted at all to the idea  of politicians  trying to stitch up the choices  available to voters in their own  localities voters are smart  voters will take their own decision  amisham they they took the decision they  took and in the west of england they  took a different decision  the key for labor uh is to win a  majority across the country  the blue wall is clearly crumbling now  but we saw in hartlepool the labour  party is having problems still  in some parts of former industrial areas  in different parts of the country  we need to build out now a policy and a  program that has broad reach across the  whole of the country  to secure the election of a labour  majority government that  is what we are focused on not stitching  up deals with other parties to deprive  voters of choice  so for you it's a labor majority or  nothing no alliances with the snp  no understandings with the liberal  democrats  well this is you know